<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Volgograd</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tsaritsyn, Stalingrad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Volgograd is a city on the west bank of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, founded as the fortress of Tsaritsyn in 1589. Its defence against the Whites in 1918–1919, in which Stalin took part, led to its renaming as Stalingrad in 1925. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Battle of Stalingrad</a> of 1942–1943, a decisive Soviet victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, destroyed most of the city, which was renamed Volgograd in 1961.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Volgograd lies on the western bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and covers 859.4 square kilometres. It is the administrative centre and largest city of Volgograd Oblast, divided into eight districts. The city stands at the east end of the Volga–Don Canal, opened in 1952 to link the two great rivers of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and is a major railway junction. Its climate is a hot-summer continental one with cold winters and low rainfall spread through the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The fortress of Tsaritsyn, named after the Tsaritsa River, stood near the mouth of that river and soon became the nucleus of a trading settlement. At the start of the 17th century its garrison numbered 350 to 400 people, and in 1607 it rebelled for six months against the troops of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasili-iv-of-russia">Vasili Shuisky</a>. The Streltsy stationed there were paid to defend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-trade-route">Volga trade route</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, though some joined the Cossacks themselves. The railway link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1871 connected the isolated area to the rest of the empire, and by the 1890s the economy rested on grain, naphtha, fish and salt.</p><p>Tsaritsyn came under Bolshevik control in November 1917, fell briefly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Army</a> in mid-1919 and returned to Bolshevik control in January 1920. On 10 April 1925 it was renamed Stalingrad in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, then General Secretary of the Communist Party, for his role in its defence. Under Soviet rule minorities were targeted: the only Jewish school in the area was closed in 1926, and the synagogue was closed in 1929 after a campaign begun in 1928. The Alexander Nevsky Cathedral, consecrated in 1918, was closed in 1929, used as a motor depot and demolished in 1932.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Battle of Stalingrad</a>, the largest and bloodiest battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, earned the city the title of Hero City. On 10 November 1961 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>'s administration renamed it Volgograd, after the river, as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinization</a>. The change was controversial because of the city's importance as a symbol of resistance, and proposals to restore the old name were floated in 1984 but not accepted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Volgograd has a little over one million inhabitants and remains an industrial city, with shipbuilding, oil refining, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and aluminium production and heavy machinery. The Mamayev Kurgan memorial complex, the hill where some of the most intense fighting took place, is dominated by The Motherland Calls, an 85-metre statue. The Panorama Museum preserves Gerhardt's Mill in its bombed-out state, and Pavlov's House, another surviving monument of the battle, stands nearby. The Alexander Nevsky Cathedral was rebuilt as a replica and consecrated in 2021.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Volgograd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final German forces in <b>Stalingrad</b>'s northern pocket laid down their arms.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet forces attacked the weak Romanian-held flanks north and south of <b>Stalingrad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-19">19 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Krasnov allied with Germany, from which he obtained weapons, and organised a sizeable Don army that besieged <b>Tsaritsyn</b> in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/don-republic">Don Republic</a></li></ul></section>
